-- title:
--   Induction step for the number of cone types controlling lattice connectivity
-- statement:
--   Work in $\mathbb{R}^d$ with the Euclidean structure, and let $\mathbb{Z}^d$ denote the integer lattice. A *configuration* $\Gamma$ assigns to every point $z \in \mathbb{R}^d$ a double cone $\Gamma(z)$, given by a unit axis $v$ and an apex angle $(\Gamma z).\mathrm{apex} \in (0,\pi/2]$, whose carrier is
--   $$(\Gamma z).\mathrm{carrier} \;=\; \tilde V\bigl(v, (\Gamma z).\mathrm{apex}\bigr) \cup \bigl(-\tilde V(v,(\Gamma z).\mathrm{apex})\bigr), \qquad \tilde V(v,\vartheta) = \Bigl\{h \ne 0 : \cos\vartheta < \tfrac{\langle v,h\rangle}{\lVert h\rVert}\Bigr\};$$
--   write $S[c] = c + S$ for the translate of a set and $V^{\Gamma}[x] = x + (\Gamma x).\mathrm{carrier}$. Then:
--
--   * $\mathrm{typesIn}(\Gamma, S) = \{(\Gamma p).\mathrm{carrier} : p \in S \cap \mathbb{Z}^d\}$, the set of cone types (a "type" being the carrier double cone itself) realised at the lattice points of $S$; its cardinality in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$ is written $\#$;
--   * $\mathrm{ConnWithin}(\Gamma, T, a, b)$ is the reflexive–transitive closure of the relation "$a, b \in T$ and ($b \in V^\Gamma[a]$ or $a \in V^\Gamma[b]$)", i.e. connectedness by an edge path all of whose vertices lie in $T$;
--   * $\mathrm{RRConnected}(\Gamma, r, R, x)$ (Definition 5.3) says that *every* lattice point $y \in B_r(x)$ satisfies $\mathrm{ConnWithin}\bigl(\Gamma,\ B_R(x) \cap \mathbb{Z}^d,\ x,\ y\bigr)$ — so $r$ measures how far the targets may be and $R$ how far the path may wander;
--   * $\mathrm{Jump}(W, c, \delta)$ relates $p$ to $q$ when $p, q \in W \cap \mathbb{Z}^d$, $\lVert q - c\rVert < \lVert p - c\rVert$ and $\lVert q - p\rVert < \delta$: a step between lattice points of $W$, strictly closer to the apex $c$, of length strictly less than $\delta$. $\mathrm{ReflTransGen}$ is its reflexive-transitive closure, i.e. a finite chain of such jumps.
--
--   Fix $\vartheta$ with $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, and reals $\delta > 0$, $t_0 > 0$. Assume the **descent hypothesis**: for every unit vector $v$, every aperture $\vartheta_V$ with $\vartheta \le \vartheta_V \le \pi/2$, every apex $c \in \mathbb{R}^d$, and every lattice point $x$ lying in the shifted one-sided cone $c + \tilde V(v,\vartheta_V)$, there is a lattice point $y$ in the same shifted cone with
--   $$\|y - c\| \le t_0 \quad\text{and}\quad x \;\xrightarrow{\ \mathrm{Jump}(c + \tilde V(v,\vartheta_V),\, c,\, \delta)^{*}\ }\; y,$$
--   that is, $y$ is reachable from $x$ by a finite chain of jumps of length less than $\delta$ inside that cone.
--
--   Assume further a natural number $k$ and reals $r' \le \rho' \le R'$ with $\delta < r'$ and $k \le r'$, together with the **inductive hypothesis**: for every configuration $\Gamma'$ all of whose apertures are at least $\vartheta$ (i.e. $\vartheta \le (\Gamma' z).\mathrm{apex}$ for all $z$) and every lattice point $p$,
--   $$\#\,\mathrm{typesIn}\big(\Gamma', B_{\rho'}(p)\big) \le k \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathrm{RRConnected}(\Gamma', r', R', p).$$
--
--   Then there exist reals $r \le \rho \le R$ with $\delta < r$ and $k + 1 \le r$ such that for every configuration $\Gamma'$ with all apertures at least $\vartheta$ and every lattice point $x$,
--   $$\#\,\mathrm{typesIn}\big(\Gamma', B_{\rho}(x)\big) \le k + 1 \;\Longrightarrow\; \mathrm{RRConnected}(\Gamma', r, R, x).$$
--
--   This is the inductive step of the core induction on the number of cone types: connectivity with at most $k$ types realised near a point upgrades to connectivity with at most $k+1$, at the price of larger radii. Iterating it from the base case $k = 1$ produces, for each $k$, constants $r_k \le \rho_k \le R_k$ valid for all configurations at once, which is the quantitative input the later chaining arguments consume.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The cardinality $\#\,\mathrm{typesIn}$ is `Set.encard`, valued in $\mathbb{N}\cup\{\infty\}$, so the hypothesis is a genuine bound even for infinite type sets. The quantifier order matters and is the point of the statement: the constants $r, \rho, R$ are produced *before* the configuration $\Gamma'$ is mentioned, which formalizes the assertion that they depend only on $\vartheta$, $d$, $\delta$ and $t_0$ — never on the configuration. The configuration $\Gamma$ appearing in the ambient section variables plays no role in the statement itself; only the universally quantified $\Gamma'$ does.
